<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Peopling of the Americas</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The peopling of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> is believed to have begun when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paleolithic">Paleolithic</a> hunter-gatherers entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> from northeastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a> over the Beringia land bridge, formed as sea levels fell during the Last Glacial Maximum (26,000 to 19,000 years ago). The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis</a> First theory dated the earliest human presence to the Clovis culture about 13,000 years ago, but pre-Clovis sites such as Monte Verde in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> pushed the date back. Academics generally believe that humans reached North America south of the Laurentide Ice Sheet between 15,000 and 20,000 years ago, inland or along the Pacific coast.</p></div>
